#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107083 :tutorial/content "To raise a fraction to a power, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to that power. This is known as the **power of quotient rule for exponents**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3\n$$\n\nby applying the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3=\\dfrac{2^3}{5^3}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power of quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{2}{5})^{3} &= (\\frac{2}{5}) \\cdot (\\frac{2}{5}) \\cdot (\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2}{5 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2^{3}}{5^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8abc3ed3-0ba8-5dc1-8bf8-437985614b92" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3273 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
